You're right, I was counting NetHack's game data but not Quake's. Here's
a slightly better comparison that only counts source and header files
(ignoring the build system, but counting platform-specific bits in each
case):

bobnet:/home/bob$ mount /cdrom
bobnet:/home/bob$ mkdir /tmp/q1source
bobnet:/home/bob$ cd /tmp/q1source/
bobnet:/tmp/q1source$ unzip /cdrom/quake/q1source.zip
[snip source being unarchived]
bobnet:/tmp/q1source$ wc `find . -name "*.[hc]*"`
[snip count of every file]
 197835  542048 4590693 total
bobnet:/tmp/q1source$ cd ~/src/nethack-3.4.3
bobnet:/home/bob/src/nethack-3.4.3$ wc `find . -name "*.[hc]*"`
[snip count of every file]
 267334  928727 7539794 total

(Those totals are counted in terms of lines, words, and bytes, if anyone
was wondering.)

And the Quake figure is about twice as large as it should be, since the
source zipfile includes the source for both QuakeWorld and WinQuake. The
Quake 2 source (using the same method) comes out as 178030 lines, 531931
words, and 4656089 bytes.

Clearly, NetHack uses a much better compression method on its data files
than either Quake or Quake 2. :-)

